/// Creates a DB capacity reservation. A DB capacity reservation reserves
/// compute and memory capacity for DB instances of one DB instance class
/// and one database engine. Amazon RDS reserves that capacity in each
/// Availability Zone that you specify. Amazon RDS reserves the same amount
/// of capacity in every Availability Zone in the request. You set that
/// amount with CapacityPerAvailabilityZone , and you set the Availability
/// Zones with AvailabilityZones . The total reserved capacity is
/// CapacityPerAvailabilityZone multiplied by the number of Availability
/// Zones. Specify StartDate and CommitmentDuration together to create a
/// future-dated reservation. A future-dated reservation reserves capacity
/// for a period that starts later. Amazon RDS holds that capacity for the
/// length of the commitment. A future-dated reservation has the following
/// additional constraints: DBInstanceClass must be a db.c , db.m , db.r ,
/// db.t , or db.x DB instance class. The letter after db. gives the DB
/// instance class family. For example, db.x2g.xlarge qualifies, and
/// db.z1d.xlarge doesn't. If DBInstanceClass is a db.m or db.r DB instance
/// class, it must be from generation 7 or later. The digit after the family
/// letter gives the generation. For example, db.m7g.xlarge qualifies, and
/// db.m5.xlarge doesn't. A db.c , db.t , or db.x DB instance class has no
/// generation requirement. The reserved capacity must total at least 32
/// vCPUs in each Availability Zone. Multiply CapacityPerAvailabilityZone by
/// the number of vCPUs of the DB instance class. The product must be 32 or
/// more. CommitmentDuration must be at least 14 days. For a db.x DB
/// instance class, it must be at least 84 days. StartDate must be from 5 to
/// 120 days in the future. Therefore, a DB instance class with 2 vCPUs
/// needs a CapacityPerAvailabilityZone of 16 or more, a class with 4 vCPUs
/// needs 8 or more, a class with 8 vCPUs needs 4 or more, and a class with
/// 16 vCPUs needs 2 or more. For the number of vCPUs of each DB instance
/// class, see DB instance classes in the Amazon RDS User Guide . You can't
/// modify a future-dated reservation before it starts. You can't cancel a
/// future-dated reservation in the scheduled state. You can't cancel a
/// reservation in the active state during its capacity commitment.
/// Therefore, you can't cancel a future-dated reservation from the time
/// that Amazon RDS schedules it until the commitment ends.
pub fn (c: Client) create_db_capacity_reservation(input: CreateDBCapacityReservationRequest) -> CreateDBCapacityReservationResponse ! CreateDBCapacityReservationErr uses (net.dial, clock, rand) {
